   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-statistics",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.12",
    "topic_title": "Setting Up a Test for the Difference Between Two Population Proportions",
    "stem": "Independent random samples of 100 students from School A and 200 students from School B find that 80 and 100 students, respectively, favor a later school start time. Let p₁ and p₂ be the proportions of all students at Schools A and B, respectively, who favor a later start time. A researcher wants to test whether School A has a greater population proportion than School B. Which hypotheses and pooled sample proportion correctly set up this test?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "H₀: p₁ − p₂ = 0; Hₐ: p₁ − p₂ < 0; pooled proportion = 180/300 = 0.60.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed-direction error: p₁ − p₂ < 0 represents School A having a smaller proportion, contrary to the researcher's claim. Fix: Translate the claim into an inequality using the stated order of the populations."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "H₀: p₁ − p₂ = 0; Hₐ: p₁ − p₂ > 0; pooled proportion = (0.80 + 0.50)/2 = 0.65.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Unweighted-average error: averaging the sample proportions gives both samples equal weight despite their different sizes. The pooled proportion counts all successes over all sampled students. Fix: Add the success counts, then divide by the combined sample size."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "H₀: p̂₁ − p̂₂ = 0; Hₐ: p̂₁ − p̂₂ > 0; pooled proportion = 180/300 = 0.60.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Statistic-versus-parameter error: these hypotheses concern observed sample proportions rather than the unknown population proportions. Hypothesis tests make claims about population parameters. Fix: Write hypotheses using p₁ and p₂, reserving p̂₁ and p̂₂ for sample results."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "H₀: p₁ − p₂ = 0; Hₐ: p₁ − p₂ > 0; pooled proportion = 180/300 = 0.60.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The claim that School A's proportion is greater translates to p₁ − p₂ > 0. Under the null hypothesis of equal population proportions, pooling gives one estimate from all sampled students: (80 + 100)/(100 + 200) = 0.60."
     }
    ]
   },
